// Pig entity definition (Phase 2 vertical slice)
// Full EntityDef wrapper with transform and state metadata.
//
// Proportions are driven by what the viewport shows, not by the numbers: see
// `cargo run -p editor --example render_png`, which dumps the marched silhouette from six angles.
// The grammar has no translate node (OQ-55), so every placed part is a sphere and a leg is a column of
// them; the only primitives that may sit at the origin are `RoundedBox`, `Cylinder` and `Torus`, and the
// last two are not reachable from RON at all. A flat surface is still expressible: `Intersect(a, Plane)`
// is bounded by `a` (cull.rs takes an intersect's first-operand enclosure), so a plane may cut a face as
// long as the plane is the *second* operand. A plane anywhere else makes the scene unbounded, and
// `Stage::under` then falls back to -0.5 and stands the animal in the air.
//
// What the renders rejected, in order:
// - `RoundedBox(half: (0.22, 0.18, 0.34), r: 0.15)` — a rounded box spans `2 * (half + r)`, so this was
//   a 0.74 x 0.66 x 0.98 slab, not the 0.44 x 0.36 body the fields read as.
// - `RoundedBox(half: (0.13, 0.15, 0.32), r: 0.13)` — collapsing the rounding in X still leaves the side
//   profile a rounded rectangle: flat back, flat belly. A loaf, not an animal.
// - Two torso spheres smoothed at `k` equal to their separation merged into one egg, and a single-sphere
//   head the same height as that egg read as a snowman.
// - Hard `Union` between overlapping spheres: a cluster of balloons with a V-notch at every join, because
//   a plain union takes the minimum and keeps the crease.
// - One sphere per leg read as a ball under the belly, and a head joined at the body's own height read
//   as a hump on the back rather than a head.
// - The first sphere-only pass read as a hippo: three fat body spheres as tall as they were long, legs
//   that were two wide balls each sitting directly under a belly that nearly touched the floor, and a
//   head the size of the torso merged into the chest. The `low` view made it unmistakable — four teats
//   under a ball, not four legs under an animal.
// - The corrective pass fixed the detachment but not the stance. Two spheres per leg reach only as far
//   below the belly as their own diameter, so the column was 0.36 long against a 0.61 animal: a stump.
//   `low` still read as teats, and `three_quarter` showed a head whose crown came nearly level with the
//   back, which is the one silhouette a pig never has.
//
// So the second pass is stance and neck, not new parts: each leg is a three-sphere column that reaches
// well below a belly lifted to y ~0.10, the legs sit outside the body's own width so they read in front
// view, and the head is smaller than the torso and carried *below* the topline on a neck thinner than
// both. The snout is low on the face, and the ears are small enough to read as ears rather than a
// bear's. Every join is a smooth union with `k` under the overlap so it reads as flesh, except the leg
// columns' joints: the third pass below found their overlap thinner than their `k`, and blending them
// further — not less — is what made them read as one column.
//
// - A third pass followed the side view: the crown sat at chest height and the snout near the knee, so
//   the animal read as grazing, not standing. Every head part moved up together — head +0.075, neck
//   +0.02, snout +0.075, nostrils with it — and the ears +0.05, because a head raised 0.075 would
//   otherwise swallow them: an ear is visible only where its sphere leaves the head's own, and at the
//   old height a raised head would have buried both. The crown now sits ~0.10 under the topline rather
//   than ~0.18.
// - The same pass read the legs as three beads, not a column: adjacent spheres are 0.135 apart with
//   r 0.082, so they overlap by 0.029 — less than the joint's k of 0.07 — and the silhouette pinched
//   0.018 at every join. Raising k to 0.12 widens the waist to the sphere's own radius without
//   lengthening the leg or adding a node, and the column reads smooth from `side` and `low`.
//
// - A fourth pass adds the marks the eye actually uses to name a pig, because the animal was a smooth
//   pink blob that happened to have a snout. Each one is geometry, not a new primitive:
//   · Eyes: a dish cut into the head with the eyeball unioned back into it. A ball alone is a bump on a
//     monochrome head; the shadowed socket rim is what reads as an eye.
//   · Ears: a three-sphere cone leaning up and outward, replacing a ball that sat on the skull like a
//     second head. The tip clears the topline, which is where a pig's ears live in silhouette.
//   · Snout: the two-sphere muzzle is cut flat by a plane, giving the disc a pig is named for. The
//     nostril cutters moved onto that face, so they are dimples in the disc rather than holes in a ball.
//   · Tail: a six-sphere loop instead of a nub. It curls in the Y-Z plane, so `side` shows the spiral
//     and `front` shows a thin line — the two views that disagreed about the old tail.
//   · Hooves: each foot is two spheres hard-unioned (the crease between them is the cloven cleft) with
//     the sole cut flat by a plane. The column stops above the sole so the widened hoof is what reaches
//     the floor; a leg that ends in its own bottom sphere reads as standing on a ball.
//   · The neck thickened to 0.125 and a jowl sphere was added under the head. The side view's deep
//     shoulder notch was a thin neck with nothing behind it; a pig carries a full throat.
//
// - A fifth pass judged the fourth's new marks from the profiles the first four were not read in:
//   · Ears: `side` showed two upright blades — a rabbit's ears or a pair of horns — because the cone's
//     tip sat above and *behind* its base, so the profile was a spike on the crown. The tip moved
//     forward over the eye and down until it sits under the crown, the base dropped with it, and the
//     flattening slab widened to 0.08 and shifted to cover the leaning cone. The front view keeps its
//     broad leaf; the side view now reads a triangle falling forward, which is what a pig wears.
//   · Hooves: the sole cut was 0.017 above the toe spheres' bottoms, so the profile was very nearly a
//     full sphere and every leg ended in a ball. The cut is 0.04 above now — a walled half-dome with a
//     flat base — and the hoof is attached to a *narrower* fetlock at a hard crease, which is the ring
//     that names an ankle. The first attempt at this used toes r 0.09 hard-unioned to the old column:
//     the foot read as a bell-bottom, because the toes were 70% wider than the leg. The finished foot
//     is only slightly wider than its own fetlock, and the column's lower blend dropped from k 0.12 to
//     0.08 so the taper survives instead of being smoothed away.
//   Both are single-node re-centrings and a cut-plane offset: the node count and depth are unchanged.
//
// - A sixth pass went after the profile, which is the view the first five were tuned by and the one the
//   head still failed: at `side` the animal read as a dog. Three causes, and only one of them was the
//   snout's size.
//   · There is no nose bridge. Skull-then-muzzle is two circles, so the top line dips concave between
//     them — a stop — and a muzzle of any radius reads as a ball stuck under the face. The fix is the
//     same shape the legs and the tail already use: a chain of shrinking spheres whose tops lie on one
//     line. A bridge sphere (r 0.115 at y 0.30, z -0.60) sits between the skull's crown and the snout
//     disc, and the profile is now one continuous slope — a pig's roman nose.
//   · The ear's flattening slab was cutting along the cone's own axis, not across it. Its normal
//     (0.643, 0, -0.766) came within 1.2 degrees of parallel to the base→tip direction, so the 0.08
//     band did not thin the ear at all: it sliced a puck out of the middle of the cone and left a thick
//     wall with a scoop in its front. The normal is now perpendicular to that axis, which keeps the
//     cone's whole length and shaves its width, and the ear reads as a leaf.
//   · The eye's dish was measured against the skull, and the bridge is what the eye now sits on. The
//     old cutter left a 0.016-deep, 0.034-wide hollow in a surface that had moved 0.0228 outward, so
//     the eyeball drowned in it. At r 0.034 the dish is 0.011 deep with a 0.025 rim.
//   The bridge is one level of depth, and that is what it cost: at 15 the editor's own re-parent begins
//   to refuse (`graft_part` answers "SmoothUnion is 17 levels deep, past the grammar's cap of 16",
//   because lifting a branch onto a deep route adds two levels), so the ear's third sphere paid for it.
//   74 nodes and depth 14, unchanged — the head's budget was moved, not grown.
//   What the renders rejected on the way: the first bridge had the muzzle at r 0.088 and y 0.285, which
//   fixed the profile and turned the front view into a frog — a disc 0.207 wide whose top stood above
//   the eye line, so the eyes disappeared into the snout. The accepted disc is 0.184 wide, spans
//   y 0.205 to 0.325, and leaves the eyes clear above it.
//
//   Note on the instrument: at `side` the whole head is ~80 px of a 480-wide frame, which is enough to
//   see that a snout is wrong and not enough to see why. Each judgement below the fifth pass was made
//   on a crop of that corner, which any viewer can make of `pig_side.png`.
//
// Hypothesis for docs/04, not a measurement: every blended join is a place the marcher cannot skip,
// since ADR-024's k-aware skip refuses exactly the large-`k` case, so an authored creature is an R1
// expense per part. Nothing here controls for that yet — the debug CPU raster's timings swing by more
// than the effect under machine load, so the claim stays a hypothesis until someone times a hard-union
// twin of this tree. docs/16 § Visual authoring records what the instrument did print for this tree,
// and why it is not the price of a blend.

EntityDef(
    shape: SmoothUnion(
        // Tail blended into the rump; the only root-level growth left, since the ears moved onto the head.
        k: 0.04,
        a: SmoothUnion(
            // Legs, blended into the body at the shoulder and hip.
            k: 0.05,
            a: SmoothUnion(
                k: 0.06,
                a: SmoothUnion(
                    // Body: rump and chest either side of a mid sphere lifted to arch the back. Spread
                    // along Z so the torso is longer than it is tall, and raised so the belly clears
                    // the floor the legs stand on by more than a leg's own width.
                    k: 0.11,
                    a: Sphere(
                        r: 0.22,
                        centre: (0.0, 0.30, 0.26),
                    ),
                    b: SmoothUnion(
                        k: 0.11,
                        a: Sphere(
                            r: 0.215,
                            centre: (0.0, 0.33, 0.03),
                        ),
                        b: Sphere(
                            r: 0.19,
                            centre: (0.0, 0.29, -0.19),
                        ),
                    ),
                ),
                b: SmoothUnion(
                    // The neck is now as thick as the throat it hides. At r 0.125 it leaves a dip of
                    // 0.02 behind the crown instead of the old 0.045 V, and the jowl below it fills the
                    // underside rather than leaving the head on a stalk.
                    k: 0.06,
                    a: Sphere(
                        r: 0.125,
                        centre: (0.0, 0.30, -0.33),
                    ),
                    b: SmoothUnion(
                        k: 0.05,
                        a: Sphere(
                            // Jowl: a dewlap under the jaw, hanging 0.065 past both the head and the
                            // neck on its own. It is what turns the head-neck-chest line into a wedge.
                            r: 0.11,
                            centre: (0.0, 0.235, -0.405),
                        ),
                        b: Union(
                            // Eyes are added after the sockets are cut, so the ball sits in the dish
                            // instead of being shaved by it. A plain `Union`, not a blend: the contact
                            // edge around an eyeball is supposed to be a crease.
                            a: Subtract(
                                kept: SmoothUnion(
                                    // Head with its ears and its flat-cut snout, in that order: the
                                    // sockets are cut after both, so a dish can never be re-filled by a
                                    // later blend.
                                    k: 0.035,
                                    a: SmoothUnion(
                                        // The nose bridge: the sphere that turns skull-then-muzzle from
                                        // two balls into one wedge. Without it the profile line dips
                                        // concave between the crown and the snout — a dog's stop — and
                                        // the muzzle, however large, reads as a ball stuck under the
                                        // face. Its top sits on the straight line from the skull's crown
                                        // to the snout disc, which is a pig's roman nose.
                                        k: 0.05,
                                        a: SmoothUnion(
                                            k: 0.03,
                                            a: Sphere(
                                                r: 0.15,
                                                centre: (0.0, 0.315, -0.50),
                                            ),
                                            b: Mirror(
                                                // Ear: a cone of shrinking spheres, then a slab cut that
                                                // flattens it across the cone. The cone alone read as a
                                                // horn — a pig's ear is a *blade*, and no union of spheres
                                                // can be thin. The two cut planes are nested inside the
                                                // ear's own `Intersect`, whose first operand keeps the
                                                // enclosure bounded. The base sits inside the skull, so
                                                // only the leaf grows out of the head.
                                                axis: 0,
                                                child: Intersect(
                                                    a: SmoothUnion(
                                                        // Two spheres, not three: the nose bridge below is
                                                        // worth more to the silhouette than this cone's
                                                        // third step, and the tree's depth is the budget
                                                        // they share. `MAX_TREE_DEPTH` is 16 and a
                                                        // re-parent costs two levels on top of the
                                                        // authored tree, so the pig has to ship at 14 —
                                                        // see docs/16 § Visual authoring.
                                                        k: 0.03,
                                                        a: Sphere(
                                                            r: 0.075,
                                                            centre: (0.1, 0.355, -0.47),
                                                        ),
                                                        b: Sphere(
                                                            r: 0.04,
                                                            centre: (0.135, 0.398, -0.505),
                                                        ),
                                                    ),
                                                    b: Intersect(
                                                        // Slab 0.08 thick, with its thin direction
                                                        // *across* the cone rather than along it: the
                                                        // normal here is perpendicular to the base→tip
                                                        // axis, so the band keeps the cone's whole
                                                        // length and shaves its width. The previous
                                                        // normal ran almost parallel to that axis, which
                                                        // did not flatten the ear at all — it sliced a
                                                        // 0.08 puck out of the middle of the cone and
                                                        // left a thick wall with a scoop in its front.
                                                        a: Plane(
                                                            normal: (0.659, 0.0, 0.752),
                                                            offset: -0.2472,
                                                        ),
                                                        b: Plane(
                                                            normal: (-0.659, 0.0, -0.752),
                                                            offset: 0.3272,
                                                        ),
                                                    ),
                                                ),
                                            ),
                                        ),
                                        b: Sphere(
                                            r: 0.115,
                                            centre: (0.0, 0.30, -0.60),
                                        ),
                                    ),
                                    b: Subtract(
                                        // Snout: two spheres smoothed sideways into the wide muzzle, then
                                        // cut flat at z = -0.69. The cut is the whole joke — a pig is a
                                        // disc on a face — and it is legal here only because the plane is
                                        // the second operand of an `Intersect`, which keeps the sphere
                                        // pair's enclosure.
                                        kept: Intersect(
                                            a: SmoothUnion(
                                                k: 0.03,
                                                a: Sphere(
                                                    r: 0.078,
                                                    centre: (0.032, 0.265, -0.720),
                                                ),
                                                b: Sphere(
                                                    r: 0.078,
                                                    centre: (-0.032, 0.265, -0.720),
                                                ),
                                            ),
                                            // Cut 0.050 out from each sphere's pole, where the two
                                            // cross-sections still overlap by 0.056: nearer the front
                                            // pinches the disc into a figure eight, nearer the centre
                                            // makes the face a cylinder.
                                            b: Plane(
                                                normal: (0.0, 0.0, -1.0),
                                                offset: 0.770,
                                            ),
                                        ),
                                        // Nostrils: shallow dimples pressed into the flat face, set just
                                        // in front of it so they remove 6.5 mm of snout and nothing else.
                                        cutter: Mirror(
                                            axis: 0,
                                            child: Sphere(
                                                r: 0.014,
                                                centre: (0.033, 0.265, -0.7775),
                                            ),
                                        ),
                                    ),
                                ),
                                cutter: Mirror(
                                    // Socket: the dish is measured against the nose bridge, not the
                                    // skull — the bridge's own surface now stands 0.0228 inside this
                                    // cutter's centre, so the old r 0.044 carved a 0.016-deep hollow
                                    // with a 0.034 rim and the eye drowned in it. At 0.034 the dish is
                                    // 0.011 deep with a 0.025 rim: still a shadow ring, no longer a
                                    // wound.
                                    axis: 0,
                                    child: Sphere(
                                        r: 0.034,
                                        centre: (0.1174, 0.3684, -0.6227),
                                    ),
                                ),
                            ),
                            b: Mirror(
                                // Eyeball: on the socket's own axis, its centre 0.008 outside the
                                // bridge's surface so the dish holds it and proud of the flesh by
                                // 0.015, which is the highlight.
                                axis: 0,
                                child: Sphere(
                                    r: 0.018,
                                    centre: (0.0954, 0.3556, -0.6185),
                                ),
                            ),
                        ),
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            b: Mirror(
                // Two authored legs, mirrored about X into four. Each is a three-sphere column that
                // stops at the ankle, then a hoof. The authored pair sits at x = 0.14, outside the
                // body's own half-width, so the front view shows legs at the sides of the animal rather
                // than four bumps under one belly.
                axis: 0,
                child: Union(
                    a: SmoothUnion(
                        // Front leg. The two cannon spheres share one radius: a taper shows as a ring
                        // at every joint, because a blend can hide a change of position but not a
                        // change of width. The fetlock and hoof are where the width changes, and the
                        // hard creases there give it an edge to happen on.
                        k: 0.08,
                        a: SmoothUnion(
                            k: 0.12,
                            a: Sphere(
                                r: 0.082,
                                centre: (0.14, 0.135, -0.16),
                            ),
                            b: Sphere(
                                r: 0.082,
                                centre: (0.14, 0.0, -0.16),
                            ),
                        ),
                        b: Union(
                            // The hoof meets the fetlock at a hard crease: a smoothed joint erased the
                            // ankle line, and a leg that fades into its own foot reads as a ball on
                            // the floor rather than a hoof. The crease is the ring that names the
                            // ankle; the smooth union is kept for the column's joints above it.
                            a: Sphere(
                                // Ankle: narrower than the cannon above, so the hoof below can flare
                                // without the whole leg reading as a bell. The taper is the fetlock.
                                r: 0.07,
                                centre: (0.14, -0.115, -0.16),
                            ),
                            b: Intersect(
                                // Hoof: two toe spheres, hard-unioned. The crease where they meet is
                                // the cloven cleft. The sole is cut 0.04 above the spheres' bottoms,
                                // so the profile is a walled half-dome with a flat base, not a full
                                // sphere; a cut nearer the bottom left the foot reading as a ball.
                                // Slightly wider than the ankle in X and Z, no wider than that.
                                a: Union(
                                    a: Sphere(
                                        r: 0.075,
                                        centre: (0.1, -0.175, -0.16),
                                    ),
                                    b: Sphere(
                                        r: 0.075,
                                        centre: (0.18, -0.175, -0.16),
                                    ),
                                ),
                                // Flat sole: inside is `y > -0.215`, so the normal points down. The
                                // plane only touches the hoof, and the column above it stops clear.
                                b: Plane(
                                    normal: (0.0, -1.0, 0.0),
                                    offset: 0.215,
                                ),
                            ),
                        ),
                    ),
                    b: SmoothUnion(
                        // Back leg: same column, under the rump, a touch wider to carry the haunch.
                        k: 0.08,
                        a: SmoothUnion(
                            k: 0.12,
                            a: Sphere(
                                r: 0.09,
                                centre: (0.14, 0.125, 0.28),
                            ),
                            b: Sphere(
                                r: 0.09,
                                centre: (0.14, 0.0, 0.28),
                            ),
                        ),
                        b: Union(
                            a: Sphere(
                                // Fetlock, matching the front leg's taper.
                                r: 0.078,
                                centre: (0.14, -0.115, 0.28),
                            ),
                            b: Intersect(
                                a: Union(
                                    a: Sphere(
                                        r: 0.082,
                                        centre: (0.095, -0.175, 0.28),
                                    ),
                                    b: Sphere(
                                        r: 0.082,
                                        centre: (0.185, -0.175, 0.28),
                                    ),
                                ),
                                b: Plane(
                                    normal: (0.0, -1.0, 0.0),
                                    offset: 0.215,
                                ),
                            ),
                        ),
                    ),
                ),
            ),
        ),
        b: SmoothUnion(
            // Tail: a loop of six shrinking spheres, climbed from the rump and tucked back under. The
            // earlier nub was a ball the size of an ear; a curl is the silhouette a pig is drawn with.
            // Overlaps are kept positive so `k` only fillets the joints, never bridges a gap.
            k: 0.025,
            a: Sphere(
                r: 0.03,
                centre: (0.0, 0.38, 0.465),
            ),
            b: SmoothUnion(
                k: 0.025,
                a: Sphere(
                    r: 0.025,
                    centre: (0.008, 0.42, 0.5),
                ),
                b: SmoothUnion(
                    k: 0.025,
                    a: Sphere(
                        r: 0.021,
                        centre: (0.018, 0.443, 0.536),
                    ),
                    b: SmoothUnion(
                        k: 0.025,
                        a: Sphere(
                            r: 0.018,
                            centre: (0.028, 0.431, 0.568),
                        ),
                        b: SmoothUnion(
                            k: 0.025,
                            a: Sphere(
                                r: 0.015,
                                centre: (0.034, 0.407, 0.583),
                            ),
                            b: Sphere(
                                r: 0.013,
                                centre: (0.034, 0.385, 0.573),
                            ),
                        ),
                    ),
                ),
            ),
        ),
    ),
    position: (0.0, 0.0, 0.0),
    rotation: (0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0),
    scale: 1.0,
    material_id: 0, // pig_skin slot
    flags: 3,       // ALIVE | ACTIVE
    quantity: 0,
)
